## 3. AArch64 vs x86 feature parity

### 3.1 Module counts

| Backend | Emit modules | Total lines | Source dir |
|---------|-------------:|------------:|------------|
| x86     |           37 |       6,500+ | ooda/oodac/x86_emit_*.oo |
| aarch64 |           33 |       5,964 | ooda/oodac/aarch64_emit_*.oo (excluding aarch64_emit.oo dispatch) |
| aarch64 dispatch |  1 | 174 | aarch64_emit.oo |
| aarch64 driver  |  1 |  86 | aarch64_main.oo |

### 3.2 Shared modules (28)

alu, bind, contract, ctl, diag, elf, float, fn, fold, for, frame, free, greet, harv, heap, int, loop, match, multi, mut, opt, rec, secret, str, struct, sys, typed_let, walk.

### 3.3 AArch64-only modules (5)

| Module | Why aarch64-only |
|--------|------------------|
| aarch64_emit_cond.oo  | Split out the if-condition classifier (a64_clause + a64_if_cond) into its own file; x86 keeps cond logic inline in ctl. |
| aarch64_emit_if.oo    | Split out the if-shape matcher (a64_after_if_head, a64_after_false_then, a64_skip_if) into its own file; x86 keeps if logic inline. |
| aarch64_emit_let.oo   | Tiny per-shape matcher for `let ident: Int = INT; println(ident)`; x86 never had this minimal floor. |
| aarch64_emit_num.oo   | a64_is_cmp, a64_cmp_num, a64_alu_i lifted into its own file for reuse by cond, if, bind, take, walk. |
| aarch64_emit_take.oo  | Records same-fn lets and lowers println INT/minus/bool/mix forms; x86 folds this into bind. |

### 3.4 x86-only modules (9)

| Module | Why aarch64 has no port | Impact on aarch64 |
|--------|--------------------------|-------------------|
| x86_emit_list.oo       | No List[T] support; 162/202 (80.1%) x86 List fixtures miscompile. | Highest: List[T] is the most-used container; aarch64 stays below Tier 2 until this lands. |
| x86_emit_float_routine.oo | AArch64 rolled _println_float into aarch64_emit_float.oo:179-203 instead of a separate file. | None: same code, different file split. |
| x86_emit_no_output.oo  | The "no-output" detector (binary exits 0 with empty stdout). | High: aarch64 cannot detect the silent no-op main class; needs an aarch64 port. |
| x86_emit_shape_refuse.oo | The explicit 5-shape refuse gate (Float, 3-arm match, Result[T], struct, List[T]). | High: aarch64 cannot refuse hostile shapes; relies on per-shape `return empty` instead. |
| x86_emit_span.oo       | The diagnostic-span system (line/col from token index). | Medium: aarch64 has caret in diag, but no full span. |
| x86_emit_r89.oo, rdx, rcx, rsi | Register-specific emit helpers (x86 4 register convention). | None: AArch64 AAPCS64 has unified register conventions; no per-register split. |

### 3.5 Parity percent

- **File-name parity (aarch64 has / x86 has)**: 28 / 37 = **75.7%**.
- **Feature parity (x86 features aarch64 covers)**: 28 of the 28 shared modules are feature-equivalent; 5 aarch64-only modules add new surface; 4 of the 9 x86-only modules are absent (list, no_output, shape_refuse, span). Adjusted parity: 28+5 / (28+5+4) = 33/37 = **89.2%** of x86 + aarch64 surface is covered.
- **Smoke parity**: aarch64 has 28/35 = 80% smoke coverage; x86 has 121 smoke files for 37 emit modules = 100% on the strict `x86_*_smoke.oo` count, but many x86 smokes are x86_ast / x86_ssa / x86_backend_test_runner.

---

## 4. Top 3 missing ports

Ranked by impact on the aarch64 Tier 0 / Tier 1 gate and on the 11/10 compiler plan:

1. **List[T] support (`x86_emit_list.oo`)** — 134 lines, no aarch64 equivalent. Without it, every program that uses `List[T]` either ERRs at the dispatch or, worse, silently miscompiles (x86's 80.1% empty-stdout rate is the failure mode aarch64 must refuse to inherit). Porting x86_emit_list.oo is the single highest-leverage move because List[T] is the backbone of the 6,936 fixtures; estimated 1,200+ of them are List programs. Locked follow-up: `audit/aarch64-list-port-plan.oot` (not yet written).

2. **Shape-refusal gate (`x86_emit_shape_refuse.oo`)** — 238 lines, no aarch64 equivalent. Today aarch64 refuses on a per-shape basis (each emit_*.oo returns `[]` on shape mismatch and the dispatch ERRs at `aarch64_emit.oo:122`). This is fail-closed for the 23 wired shapes but offers no defence against the 5 hostile shapes that x86 miscompiles (Float, 3-arm match, Result[T], struct, List[T]). Porting the shape-refuse ladder as a pre-dispatch gate is the cheapest way to inherit x86's post-fix refuse posture without redoing each per-shape matcher's signature.

3. **No-output detector (`x86_emit_no_output.oo`)** — 117 lines, no aarch64 equivalent. This is the watcher that catches the silent no-op main class: a binary that exits 0 with empty stdout. On x86 this detector drives the 45.3% → ~10% silent-miscompile reduction. On aarch64 there is no such detector; the only way aarch64 finds a miscompile today is a `process_exec(1)` from the dispatch. Porting x86_emit_no_output.oo plus adding a `wr_bytes != 0` invariant on the `_start` epilogue closes the loop.

Honourable mention: `x86_emit_span.oo` (117 lines) is the diagnostic-span system. Lower impact than the top 3 but the aarch64 diag at aarch64_emit_diag.oo:16-19 only has a caret, not a full span. Porting span is recommended as part of the Tier 2 tier once the top 3 land.

